"""Result-independent manuscript sections (Introduction, Related Work, Methods). Parameter values here are the executed configuration."""


def intro(M):
    M.h(1, '1. Introduction')
    M.h(2, '1.1 Clinical and technical motivation')
    M.p("Dermoscopy improves the diagnosis of pigmented skin lesions relative to unaided inspection, and deep neural networks have reached dermatologist-level accuracy in selected, controlled settings [@esteva2017,haenssle2018,tschandl2019,liu2020,tschandl2020]. HAM10000 made a multi-source, seven-class dermoscopic benchmark widely available [@ham10000], and accuracy-type metrics on such benchmarks have become the default currency of progress. Yet aggregate discrimination says little about clinical trustworthiness: an accurate classifier can have miscalibrated probabilities, spatially unreliable explanations, different behaviour across skin tones, or collapse on images from another acquisition setting [@ghassemi2021,vandervelden2022,hauser2022].")
    M.p("Dermoscopic images make these questions concrete: lesions occupy from a few percent to most of the frame, are surrounded by hair, rulers, markings and vignettes, and datasets carry acquisition artefacts that models can exploit instead of lesion morphology [@bissoto2019,winkler2019]. Whether a highlighted region reflects the evidence the model used, whether it lands on the lesion, and how much of its position is dictated by lesion geometry rather than model behaviour are empirical questions that need quantitative controls, not visual plausibility.")
    M.h(2, '1.2 Explainability as a measurable object')
    M.p("Grad-CAM yields a coarse class-specific map from gradients into a convolutional layer, and Grad-CAM++ refines the weighting for small or multiple objects [@gradcam,gradcampp]. Saliency maps can nevertheless be insensitive to model parameters and fail quantitative tests [@adebayo2018,samek2017,hooker2019,yeh2019], so evaluation must separate __localization__ (does the map overlap a reference region such as a lesion mask), __faithfulness__ (does the prediction depend on the regions marked important) and __usefulness__ (does it help a clinician). The fraction of saliency mass inside a segmentation mask ('relevance mass accuracy') is a standard localization measure [@arras2022]; here it is called the Lesion-Saliency Alignment Score (LSAS), a localization measure only, neither faithfulness nor evidence of clinical utility.")
    M.h(2, '1.3 Calibration, fairness and dataset shift')
    M.p("Calibration differs from discrimination: a model can rank classes well while its confidences are wrong, and temperature scaling, summarized by the expected calibration error (ECE) and Brier score, is a simple remedy [@guo2017,naeini2015,brier1950]. Under dataset shift, accuracy and calibration can both degrade sharply [@ovadia2019]. Dermatology datasets under-represent darker skin, with documented weaker performance for darker tones and rarer conditions [@adamson2018,daneshjou2022,groh2021,groh2024,wen2022]. Prediction fairness and explanation fairness differ, and a non-significant test in a sparse subgroup is not evidence of fairness.")
    M.h(2, '1.4 Study objective and contributions')
    M.p("This study audits a DenseNet121 dermoscopic classifier (primary model), compares it with a ResNet50 and with INT8-quantized deployment versions of both, and examines each along eight trust dimensions using the pipeline of [[F:pipeline]]: diagnostic performance, calibration, explanation localization, explanation faithfulness, explanation stability, lesion-size dependence, skin-tone-proxy variation and external generalization. It proposes no new architecture, training procedure or saliency method; its contribution is a null-controlled evaluation framework, built around one new metric, that separates measurement artefacts from model behaviour (claims N1-N4 below). All analyses are repeated over five seeds under a fully annealed schedule with a pre-specified checkpoint rule and cover correctly classified and misclassified images alike; the skin-tone proxy is reported with uncertainty and power analysis rather than as a fairness claim; the PAD-UFES-20 drop is decomposed and target-domain adaptation tested (Section 4.9); and the architectures and their INT8 versions are compared under a decision rule fixed before any test-set evaluation (Sections 3.4, 3.14-3.15).")
    M.h(2, '1.5 What is new and what is reused')
    M.p("__Novelty claim.__ The study makes four bounded claims and no others; it proposes no new classifier, saliency method, quantization method or dataset. __(N1) Geometric control of mask-overlap localization.__ When saliency location is independent of the mask, the expected relevance-mass score equals the mask-area fraction however concentrated the map (Section 3.8, analytically and by simulation on real masks); the excess over a cross-image shuffled-saliency null (excess LSAS), with shift-null, resolution-ceiling and permutation controls, is the quantity to interpret. __(N2) Null-controlled faithfulness.__ The excess insertion and deletion gain (EIG, EDG; Section 3.9) uses the Grad-CAM++ explanation of a different image as null, testing whether an explanation is specific to its own image and not merely better than a random patch order; the claim is limited to the architectures and layer tested (Sections 4.11-4.12). __(N3) Exactly additive external-validation decomposition.__ The internal-to-external accuracy drop is split into population, label-space, prevalence and residual components that sum exactly to the total (Section 3.12, Equation 3). __(N4) Paired multi-criterion model comparison.__ Two architectures and their INT8 versions are compared on identical data, five seeds each, on classification, calibration, external generalization, explanation reliability and efficiency under a decision rule fixed in advance (Section 3.15); the INT8 component applies established methods [@jacob2018,krishnamoorthi2018,nagel2021] and quantization effects on saliency maps have been studied [@kerkouri2024,rabbi2026], so the contribution is the joint audit with null-controlled explanation metrics, not the quantization technique.")
    M.table(['Component', 'Status', 'Origin or prior art', 'Role in this study', 'Specified in'], [
        ['ImageNet-pretrained DenseNet121 backbone', 'Reused', 'Huang et al. [@huang2017]', 'Primary classifier (M1)', 'Section 3.4'],
        ['ImageNet-pretrained ResNet50 backbone', 'Reused', 'He et al. [@he2016]', 'Secondary classifier (M2)', 'Section 3.4'],
        ['Focal plus label-smoothed cross-entropy, class-balanced sampler, AdamW, one-cycle schedule', 'Reused', '[@lin2020,loshchilov2019,smith2019]', 'Identical training recipe for M1 and M2', 'Section 3.4'],
        ['Temperature scaling, ECE, Brier score', 'Reused', '[@guo2017,naeini2015,brier1950]', 'Calibration', 'Section 3.6'],
        ['Grad-CAM++ explanation', 'Reused', 'Chattopadhay et al. [@gradcampp]', 'Explanation method for M1 and M2', 'Section 3.7'],
        ['Relevance-mass localization score (LSAS)', 'Adapted', 'Relevance mass accuracy [@arras2022]', 'Localization metric against expert lesion masks', 'Section 3.7'],
        ['Geometric expectation of LSAS; shuffled, shift and ceiling nulls; excess LSAS; permutation test', 'New (N1)', 'This study', 'Separates model behaviour from lesion geometry', 'Section 3.8'],
        ['Deletion/insertion faithfulness with random-order controls', 'Reused', 'RISE protocol [@rise]', 'Perturbation faithfulness', 'Section 3.9'],
        ['Excess insertion/deletion gain (EIG, EDG)', 'New (N2)', 'This study', 'Image-specificity of an explanation', 'Section 3.9'],
        ['MC-dropout saliency stability (STS)', 'Adapted', 'MC dropout [@gal2016]', 'Explanation stability', 'Section 3.10'],
        ['Individual Typology Angle skin-tone proxy', 'Reused', '[@chardon1991,delbino2013]', 'Skin-tone-proxy sensitivity analysis', 'Section 3.11'],
        ['Stepwise external-validation decomposition (population, label space, prevalence, residual)', 'New (N3)', 'This study, built on standard shift diagnostics [@saerens2002,morenotorres2012]', 'Explains the HAM10000 to PAD-UFES-20 drop', 'Section 3.12'],
        ['INT8 post-training static quantization and quantization-aware pilot (FX graph mode)', 'Reused', '[@jacob2018,krishnamoorthi2018,nagel2021]', 'INT8 deployment versions (M3)', 'Section 3.14'],
        ['Validation-selected INT8 configuration with stem and head kept in FP32', 'Adapted', 'Common practice for first and last layers [@nagel2021]', 'Recovers accuracy lost by naive INT8', 'Section 3.14'],
        ['Paired, multi-criterion comparison under a pre-specified decision rule', 'New (N4)', 'This study', 'Selects the best configuration per criterion', 'Section 3.15'],
    ], 'What is new and what is reused in this study.',
        "Reused = used as published; Adapted = a published method applied with the modification stated in the section cited; New = to our knowledge, on the literature reviewed in Sections 2.4-2.6 and 1.5, not previously reported for dermoscopic saliency evaluation. 'New' refers to the construction or its application, never to a component that is individually established.", label='tab:novelty')
    M.p("[[T:novelty]] itemizes every component with its status. N1 and N3 are derived or computed identically for both architectures; N2 is demonstrated for one architecture and tested, without outcome selection, on a second (Section 4.12); N4 is a design whose conclusions depend on the measured results and are reported criterion by criterion (Section 4.15).")
    M.p("__How this differs from recent dermoscopic XAI work.__ Grad-CAM-family methods are widely used in dermoscopic classifiers; recent studies compared saliency methods against clinical criteria [@giavinabianchi2023], examined morphometric properties of saliency maps as correlates of correctness [@fernandez2025], proposed harmonized multi-architecture protocols [@sonune2026] or paired ensemble classifiers with composite faithfulness/robustness/complexity scores [@khan2026]. None null-controls the mask-overlap statistic against the geometry of the reference region, so a lesion-size effect that is largely mathematical (Section 3.8) cannot be distinguished there from a genuine property of the explanation; radiology audits report a size-dependent localization gap that is consistent with, though not previously attributed to, this confound [@arun2021,saporta2022] (Section 2.4). To our knowledge no equivalent null-controlled, multi-seed audit combining localization, perturbation faithfulness, stochastic stability and a decomposition of external-domain performance exists for dermoscopic classification; Section 4.10 places the present results in the context of a 2026 evaluation-integrity audit of HAM10000 benchmarks [@jamaludin2026].")


def related(M):
    M.h(1, '2. Related Work')
    M.h(2, '2.1 Dermoscopic classification and dataset artefacts')
    M.p("Early neural approaches classified lesion images end-to-end at expert level [@esteva2017,haenssle2018], and human–machine collaboration can improve reader accuracy, though not uniformly [@tschandl2020]. Reviews emphasize heterogeneous protocols, imbalance and limited external validation [@kassem2021,hauser2022], and recent HAM10000 classifiers increasingly report a post-hoc explanation as a matter of course rather than as a distinct evaluation target [@hosny2024]. DenseNet's dense connectivity gives compact, feature-reusing backbones [@huang2017] and focal loss down-weights easy examples under imbalance [@lin2020]; both are used here as established components. Skin-lesion datasets contain artefacts classifiers can exploit, including borders, rulers and surgical markings [@bissoto2019,winkler2019], which motivates checking where a model's evidence lies.")
    M.h(2, '2.2 Evaluating explanations')
    M.p("Quantitative saliency evaluation includes sanity checks [@adebayo2018], region-perturbation curves [@samek2017], deletion and insertion metrics from RISE [@rise], retraining-based removal benchmarks [@hooker2019], infidelity and sensitivity [@yeh2019], and toolkits bundling such metrics [@arras2022]. Localization metrics measuring saliency mass inside a ground-truth region are standard [@arras2022], but removal-based faithfulness metrics have their own confound: replacing pixels moves inputs off the training distribution, which can be mistaken for importance [@hooker2019,rong2022]. Explanations of black-box models in medicine have also been criticized as unreliable substitutes for validated interpretability [@rudin2019,ghassemi2021]. Localization and faithfulness are therefore measured separately here, each against explicit random or null baselines, and their agreement is tested rather than assumed.")
    M.h(2, '2.3 Uncertainty, calibration and reproducibility')
    M.p("Monte Carlo dropout approximates predictive uncertainty by repeated stochastic forward passes [@gal2016]; here it serves only as a source of controlled perturbation for measuring saliency reproducibility. Modern networks are often over-confident and shift degrades probability quality [@guo2017,ovadia2019]. Single runs can conceal substantial between-run variance [@henderson2018,bouthillier2021], so every result is reported across five independently trained seeds. Reporting follows the spirit of CLAIM and TRIPOD+AI [@mongan2020,collins2024]; this remains an offline computational evaluation, not a clinical study [@nagendran2020,kelly2019].")
    M.h(2, '2.4 Quantitative evaluation of saliency maps in medical imaging')
    M.p("Systematic audits of saliency localization in radiology are the closest methodological precedent. An evaluation of eight saliency methods for pneumothorax and pneumonia localization on chest radiographs found that all eight failed at least one of four trustworthiness criteria (localization utility, sensitivity to weight randomization, repeatability, reproducibility) and were markedly inferior to dedicated localization networks [@arun2021]. A benchmark of seven saliency methods against a radiologist reference found that Grad-CAM localized best but still significantly worse than radiologists, with the gap largest for smaller, more complex findings and model confidence positively correlated with localization quality [@saporta2022]. Neither study, nor the dermoscopic literature above, separates a genuine localization effect from the geometric dependence of a mask-overlap score on target size; Section 3.8 does so for LSAS, and its mathematics (Equation 2) is modality-independent. Deletion/insertion metrics have also been critiqued because they use only the rank order of saliency values, so visually very different maps that reorder pixels identically receive identical scores; sparsity- and calibration-aware complements have been proposed [@gomez2022]. The present protocol (Section 3.9) reports both metrics with random- and lesion-mask-order controls rather than proposing a new one, and treats rank-order sensitivity as an explicit limitation (Section 6).")
    M.h(2, '2.5 External generalization and domain shift in dermoscopic and medical imaging AI')
    M.p("Performance loss on external data is not particular to this study or to dermoscopy. A systematic review of 86 externally validated deep-learning algorithms for radiologic diagnosis found that 81% showed some decrease in external performance, 24% by at least 0.10 on the reported scale, with no study characteristic reliably predicting its size [@yu2022ext]. Standardized protocols for quantifying covariate and concept shift remain underdeveloped [@matta2024]. In dermoscopy, the 2019 International Skin Imaging Collaboration Grand Challenge, the largest controlled comparison of skin-lesion classifiers to date (64 teams, 129 submissions), found that the best algorithm reached 82.0% balanced accuracy on HAM10000 but only 58.8% on BCN20000, a dataset curated to better reflect clinical acquisition, and that the top submissions misclassified images from categories absent from training as malignant in 47.1% of cases [@combalia2022]. Grouping ISIC-archive images by acquisition metadata (site, location, age) produces measurable domain shifts even within one archive [@fogelberg2023]. These findings do not identify what drives the drop on PAD-UFES-20 here, but they show that an internal-to-external gap of the magnitude reported (Section 4.9) is a documented, common outcome across modalities rather than an isolated weakness of this model.")
    M.h(2, '2.6 Evaluation-protocol integrity: dataset leakage and seed variance in dermoscopic benchmarks')
    M.p("Reported HAM10000 accuracies frequently exceed 90-95% under protocols that are not always fully specified. An evaluation-integrity audit of hybrid quantum-classical HAM10000 classifiers found that 22.8% of lesions (1,095 of 4,810) crossed the train/test boundary under a common image-level partitioning scheme, and that a verified lesion-level partition with five-seed evaluation reduced accuracy for the identical pipeline from 94.57% to 78.41% ± 0.74% (macro-F1 0.804); recent quantum-hybrid studies claiming above 90% were consistently associated with undisclosed or leakage-prone protocols [@jamaludin2026]. A data-quality audit of HAM10000 and Fitzpatrick17k documented duplicates, overlap and label inconsistencies [@abhishek2025], and in optical coherence tomography improper splitting alone inflated accuracy by 5 to 30 points [@tampu2022]. Macro-averaged metrics on HAM10000 under natural imbalance are far less stable than accuracy across folds and seeds (macro-F1 0.27 to 0.53 for the same architecture and split, accuracy 0.72-0.74) [@dagoglu2025], and random seed alone can produce non-trivial accuracy outliers [@picard2021]. Section 4.10 interprets the present accuracy and macro-F1 against this literature.")


def methods(M, cfg, after_data=None):
    M.h(1, '3. Materials and Methods')
    M.h(2, '3.1 Study design and evidence boundary')
    M.p("[[F:pipeline]] summarizes the pipeline: dataset partitioning and preprocessing, five-seed training and calibration of the DenseNet121 and ResNet50 classifiers, Grad-CAM++ generation, four parallel explanation-reliability audits (null-controlled localization, perturbation faithfulness, stochastic stability, skin-tone-proxy analysis), external evaluation and decomposition on PAD-UFES-20, multi-seed aggregation into the trust report of Section 4, and the INT8 deployment and model-comparison stages (Sections 3.14-3.15). Each stage is specified in Sections 3.2-3.16, and the chain from every claim to its method, test and evidence is tabulated in Section 3.17 ([[T:claims]]). All numerical results come from one executed pipeline (accompanying repository, folder revision/) run under a protocol fixed before any test-set evaluation: a fully annealed schedule for every seed, disjoint validation subsets for checkpoint selection and temperature-scaling calibration, official HAM10000 lesion masks (Section 3.2), and explanation analyses on every test image regardless of prediction correctness (Section 3.7). Five independent seeds were used throughout and every headline statistic is a five-seed quantity (mean (SD), or range and consistency count across seeds); analyses needing one set of per-image results are labelled reference-seed analyses, and no partially trained model appears in any table.")
    M.h(2, '3.2 Data and lesion-level partitioning')
    M.p(f"HAM10000 contains 10,015 dermoscopic images of 7,470 unique lesions in seven classes: actinic keratoses/intraepithelial carcinoma (AKIEC), basal cell carcinoma (BCC), benign keratosis-like lesions (BKL), dermatofibroma (DF), melanoma (MEL), melanocytic nevi (NV) and vascular lesions (VASC) [@ham10000]. Because several images can depict the same lesion, splitting was performed on lesion_id, stratified by class, in two stages (80/20, then the 20% split 50/50; scikit-learn train_test_split, random_state = 42). The split reproduces the original partition exactly (8,020 training, 1,005 validation and 990 test images; identical class counts) and is held fixed across all model seeds, so that seed-to-seed variation reflects training stochasticity and not test-set differences. The validation set was divided into two disjoint stratified halves: val_sel (502 images) for checkpoint selection and val_cal (503 images) for temperature scaling. Patient identifiers are not provided, so patient-level disjointness cannot be verified; lesion-level disjointness was enforced. The partition and the class counts of both datasets are given in [[T:dataset]].")
    M.p("Lesion masks are the expert-curated binary HAM10000 lesion segmentations (Tschandl et al.; Harvard Dataverse file HAM10000_segmentations_lesion_tschandl.zip, one mask per image) [@tschandl2020]. All 990 test images have a valid mask, so no image is excluded from any explanation analysis. Masks were resized to 224 × 224 with bilinear interpolation and binarized at 127.")
    if after_data: after_data(M)
    M.h(2, '3.3 Preprocessing and augmentation')
    M.p("A DullRazor-style hair-removal step [@lee1997] was applied at native resolution (9 × 9 black-hat filter, threshold 10, morphological opening with a 5 × 5 elliptical kernel, connected-component filtering at 8 px, 3 × 3 dilation, Telea inpainting with radius 3), followed by resizing to 224 × 224 and ImageNet normalization. Training augmentation comprised random resized crop (scale 0.7–1.0, p = 0.5), horizontal and vertical flips, rotation up to 180° (p = 0.7), colour jitter (brightness/contrast 0.2, saturation 0.1, hue 0.05; p = 0.5), Gaussian blur (p = 0.2) and coarse dropout (p = 0.3). Validation and test preprocessing was deterministic.")
    M.h(2, '3.4 Model configurations, loss and training')
    M.p("Three configurations are evaluated on identical data, splits, preprocessing, loss and checkpoint rule, so that differences are attributable to the model and not the protocol: __M1__, a DenseNet121 with Grad-CAM++ explanations (primary model, all headline results); __M2__, a ResNet50 with Grad-CAM++ (secondary model, comparing classifiers and testing whether the findings depend on the architecture); and __M3__, INT8-quantized deployment versions of M1 and M2. Grad-CAM++ is an explanation method, not a classifier, and is applied identically to M1 and M2. [[T:model_flow]] states, for each configuration, the condition before it is applied, the intervention, the expected change and where it is measured; measured before-and-after values are in Sections 4.12-4.15 ([[T:before_after]]).")
    M.p("__M1: DenseNet121 + Grad-CAM++ (primary model).__ An ImageNet-pretrained DenseNet121 [@huang2017], fine-tuned end-to-end (7.48 M parameters), whose classifier is replaced by dropout (p = 0.3), a 512-unit linear layer, batch normalization, ReLU, dropout (p = 0.2) and a seven-way linear layer, followed by temperature scaling on a disjoint validation half (Section 3.6) and the explanation audit (Sections 3.7-3.12). The starting condition is measured as a frozen-feature baseline (frozen backbone, class-balanced logistic-regression head fitted on the training split; Section 4.14).")
    M.p("__M2: ResNet50 + Grad-CAM++ (secondary model).__ The same protocol applied to an ImageNet-pretrained ResNet50 [@he2016] (24.56 M parameters; same head as M1; peak learning rates 1 × 10⁻⁵ for the stem and stages 1–2, 5 × 10⁻⁵ for stages 3–4 and 3 × 10⁻⁴ for the head, mirroring M1), five seeds, with Grad-CAM++ at the last convolution of the final bottleneck block (layer4[-1].conv3, a 7 × 7 grid as for M1). It tests which of the explanation findings of Sections 4.4-4.11, which rest on one backbone and one target layer, replicate across architectures, and allows a head-to-head comparison of the classifiers; a frozen-feature ResNet50 baseline measures the starting condition.")
    M.p("__M3: INT8 deployment versions of M1 and M2.__ Post-training static INT8 quantization with a configuration selected on validation data and, where validation scores stayed outside a pre-specified tolerance, a group-wise sensitivity analysis and a quantization-aware pilot (Section 3.14). Because gradients cannot propagate through integer kernels, explanations for an INT8 deployment come from its FP32 parent, and prediction consistency between the two is measured.")
    M.table(['Configuration', 'Role', 'Condition before', 'Intervention', 'Change expected', 'Measured in'], [
        ['M1: DenseNet121 + Grad-CAM++', 'Primary model; all headline results', 'ImageNet features not adapted to dermoscopy (frozen-feature baseline); explanations unaudited', 'End-to-end fine-tuning (focal plus label-smoothed loss, balanced sampler, one-cycle schedule); temperature scaling; null-controlled explanation audit', 'Higher discrimination than frozen features; calibrated confidence; explanation reliability separated from lesion geometry', 'Sections 4.1-4.11, 4.14'],
        ['M2: ResNet50 + Grad-CAM++', 'Secondary model; architecture comparator', 'Findings established for one backbone and one target layer', 'Identical protocol with a ResNet50 backbone; Grad-CAM++ at the final bottleneck convolution; five seeds', 'Shows which findings are architecture-general and whether either classifier is preferable', 'Sections 4.12, 4.14-4.15'],
        ['M3: INT8 versions of M1 and M2', 'Deployment configuration', 'FP32 model: four bytes per parameter, FP32 CPU speed', 'Post-training static INT8 quantization with validation-selected configuration (stem and head in FP32); group-wise sensitivity analysis and quantization-aware pilot if outside tolerance', 'About four times smaller and faster on CPU; scores within tolerance of the FP32 parent; explanations from the FP32 parent', 'Sections 4.13-4.15'],
    ], 'Model configurations: condition before, intervention applied, change expected and where the change is measured.',
        'M1 and M2 share data, split, preprocessing, loss, sampler, schedule, checkpoint rule and the five seeds; M3 is derived from the trained M1 and M2 checkpoints without access to test data.', label='tab:model_flow')
    M.p("__Loss and optimization (M1 and M2).__ The loss is the sum of a class-weighted focal loss (γ = 2; class weights = manual factors × inverse-frequency weights, normalized) and 0.1 × label-smoothed (0.1) cross-entropy [@lin2020]; training uses a class-balanced weighted sampler. Optimization uses AdamW [@loshchilov2019] (weight decay 1 × 10⁻⁴, gradient-norm clip 1.0, mixed precision) with discriminative peak learning rates (M1: 1 × 10⁻⁵ for the stem, transitions and dense blocks 1–2; 5 × 10⁻⁵ for dense blocks 3–4; 3 × 10⁻⁴ for the head; M2: as stated above) under a one-cycle schedule [@smith2019] (30% warm-up).")
    M.p("__Convergence protocol (M1 and M2).__ Each run uses a complete 60-epoch one-cycle schedule, annealing the learning rate to zero by the final epoch. The checkpoint is the epoch with the highest val_sel macro-F1 (ties broken by lower val_sel loss), a rule fixed before any test-set evaluation. Convergence is judged from (i) the training-loss and training-accuracy plateau, (ii) the slope of validation loss and F1 over the last 15 epochs, (iii) the epoch at which a patience-15 early-stopping rule would have fired, and (iv) a sensitivity run with a 100-epoch fully annealed schedule for the first seed.")
    M.h(2, '3.5 Multi-seed validation')
    M.p("The full pipeline was executed for five seeds (42, 43, 44, 45, 46) for each architecture; the two architectures' training runs are independent and seeds are shared only as labels. The seed controls the initialization of the new head, the weighted-sampler draws, the data-loader worker streams (hence the augmentation sequence), dropout masks and the random baselines of the explanation analyses; it does not alter the split. Results are mean ± SD over seeds with a t-based 95% confidence interval of the seed mean; test-sampling uncertainty is reported separately with lesion-clustered bootstrap intervals (resampling lesion_id, 500 resamples per seed). No seed was excluded or selected; seed 42 was designated the reference seed before the runs began and is used only where a single set of per-image results is required, and every headline statistic is reported for all five seeds.")
    M.h(2, '3.6 Calibration and classification metrics')
    M.p("Temperature scaling [@guo2017] fits a single scalar T by minimizing the negative log-likelihood of val_cal logits (L-BFGS); calibrated probabilities are softmax(z/T) (Equation 1). Top-label ECE uses 15 equal-width bins [@naeini2015]; the Brier score is the mean over classes of the one-vs-rest binary Brier score (the multiclass sum over all seven classes would be seven times larger) [@brier1950]. Accuracy, balanced accuracy, macro-F1, macro one-vs-rest AUROC, macro PR-AUC [@saito2015], MCC [@chicco2020] and Cohen's κ [@cohen1960] are reported.")
    M.eq('p(y = k | x) = exp(z_k / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T)', 1)
    M.h(2, '3.7 Grad-CAM++ and LSAS')
    M.p("Grad-CAM++ [@gradcampp] maps were computed with pytorch-grad-cam 1.5.x at the last convolution of dense block 4 (denseblock4.denselayer16.conv2), bilinearly upsampled to 224 × 224 and min–max normalized per image, for the model's predicted class. The Lesion-Saliency Alignment Score is the fraction of saliency mass inside the lesion mask (Equation 2), where H is the map, M the binary mask and Ω the image domain:")
    M.eq('LSAS(x) = Σ_{p∈M} H_p / Σ_{p∈Ω} H_p', 2)
    M.p("LSAS was computed for every test image, correct or not, using the predicted class as the explained class (the class the model acted on), which avoids the selection bias of restricting the analysis to correct predictions (Section 4.4). For misclassified images a secondary map for the true class was also computed. LSAS quantifies localization only.")
    M.h(2, '3.8 Geometric audit of the lesion-size effect')
    M.p("Let N = |Ω| and a = |M|/N the mask-area fraction. If saliency is spatially uniform, LSAS = a exactly; more generally, if the location of the saliency mass is independent of the mask and uniformly distributed, E[LSAS] = a irrespective of how concentrated the map is (concentration changes only the variance). A positive association between lesion size and LSAS is therefore expected __by construction__, even for maps containing no information about the lesion, and cannot be read as a behavioural effect until this expectation is subtracted. Conversely, the attainable maximum also depends on a: a Grad-CAM map from a 7 × 7 feature layer has a minimum support of about one cell (2% of the image, more after bilinear upsampling), so for smaller lesions LSAS is bounded well below 1 even for a perfectly centred map. The audit therefore uses explicit baselines:")
    M.bullets([
        "__Uniform baseline:__ LSAS_unif = a.",
        "__Cross-image shuffled-saliency null:__ for mask i, the mean LSAS obtained by pairing it with the Grad-CAM++ maps of the other 989 test images (preserves the model's spatial prior, including centre bias, while breaking the link to the image). A rank statistic (proportion of shuffled pairings below the observed LSAS) is also stored.",
        "__Toroidal-shift null:__ for each image, 100 random circular shifts of its own map against its own mask (preserves the amplitude distribution of that image's map; per-image mean, SD and a one-sided permutation p-value).",
        "__Ceiling (oracle) baseline:__ LSAS of the mask itself, average-pooled to the 7 × 7 CAM grid, bilinearly upsampled and min–max normalized, i.e. the LSAS an ideal map with exactly this layer's spatial resolution would obtain.",
        "__Area-robust localization:__ the pixel-wise ROC AUC of the map as a classifier of mask membership (0.5 under chance, independent of a by construction).",
    ])
    M.p("The area-adjusted quantity used for inference is the excess localization e = LSAS − LSAS_null (shuffled). Whether the raw association with area exceeds that expected from geometry was tested by permutation: for 2,000 random re-pairings of maps with masks (no fixed points), the Spearman correlation and the Kruskal–Wallis statistic across size strata were recomputed to build a reference distribution. Further tests: rank correlations and Kruskal–Wallis tests of e, LSAS/a, the pixel-wise AUC and LSAS/oracle against area and size stratum; OLS models of LSAS, e and AUC on log a with class fixed effects and lesion-clustered standard errors; residuals from a LOWESS fit of the null on area [@cleveland1979]; and per-stratum Wilcoxon tests of e against zero. Size strata were as originally defined (small < 5%, medium 5–15%, large ≥ 15% of image area). A model-independent simulation using the real masks and synthetic saliency maps (uniform, diffuse at random location, concentrated at random location, lesion-centred, lesion-aligned oracle) documents the mathematical expectations.")
    M.h(2, '3.9 Faithfulness: deletion and insertion')
    M.p("Faithfulness was assessed with the deletion and insertion protocol of RISE [@rise], following region-perturbation practice [@samek2017]. The 224 × 224 input is partitioned into 14 × 14 = 196 non-overlapping 16 × 16 patches, each scored by the mean of the Grad-CAM++ map. __Deletion__ replaces patches by a baseline in order of decreasing saliency and records the calibrated probability of the originally predicted class after each step (0, 14, 28, …, 196 patches removed; 15 points); __insertion__ starts from the baseline and reveals patches in the same order. The area under each curve (trapezoidal rule, x = fraction of patches) is the metric: lower deletion-AUC and higher insertion-AUC indicate a more faithful map. The primary baseline is the ImageNet-mean image (zero after normalization); a heavily blurred image (Gaussian σ = 25 px) is a sensitivity baseline, as in RISE. Controls are (i) random patch order (mean of three permutations per image), (ii) least-important-first order (which mirrors insertion by construction), and (iii) two oracle-mask orders (lesion-covered patches first vs. background patches first) testing whether the prediction depends more on the lesion than on its surroundings. The gains are DG = AUC_del(random) − AUC_del(CAM) and IG = AUC_ins(CAM) − AUC_ins(random); positive values mean the map is more informative than chance. Patch replacement moves images off the training distribution and can create artefactual probability drops [@hooker2019,rong2022]; the random order and the two baselines bound this confound, and faithfulness is interpreted only relative to them. The localization–faithfulness relation was tested by Spearman correlations of e with DG and IG, with partial correlations controlling for lesion area.")
    M.p("__Null-controlled faithfulness (new metric).__ The random-order control in DG and IG shows only that a structured revealing order outperforms no structure, not that an image's own explanation beats a plausible but wrong one. For each test image, the same deletion and insertion procedure (mean-image baseline) was repeated with the Grad-CAM++ maps of three other randomly chosen test images (already computed; no re-inference of the explainer) as the patch-importance order applied to this image's own pixels, and the mean AUC over the three donors was the null curve. The excess insertion gain is EIG = IG(observed map) − IG(cross-image null map) and the excess deletion gain is EDG = DG(cross-image null map) − DG(observed map); both are positive when the image's own explanation is more functionally informative than a mismatched explanation of similar visual character, which the random order cannot establish. Donors were drawn once per test image, without replacement and excluding the image itself, from a seed-specific random state fixed before analysis. EIG and EDG are tested against zero (Wilcoxon signed-rank) and correlated with excess LSAS (Spearman), per seed and across seeds.")
    M.h(2, '3.10 Explanation stability')
    M.p("Stochastic saliency stability (STS) was computed for a fixed random subset of 300 test images (identical for all seeds; sample(300, random_state = 42)). For each image, 15 Grad-CAM++ maps were obtained with dropout active (independent masks per pass, batch normalization in evaluation mode) for the deterministic model's predicted class, and STS is the mean pairwise Pearson correlation of the 15 flattened maps. Images with STS < 0.5 are flagged unstable (threshold defined before analysis). The same passes give the agreement of MC-dropout predictions with the deterministic prediction [@gal2016]. Stable maps are not necessarily faithful; the relation of STS to localization and faithfulness is examined explicitly.")
    M.h(2, '3.11 Skin-tone proxy (ITA) analysis')
    M.p("HAM10000 has no clinician-assigned Fitzpatrick type [@fitzpatrick1988]. The Individual Typology Angle, ITA = arctan[(L* − 50)/b*] × 180/π [@chardon1991,delbino2013], was estimated from non-lesion pixels of the 224 × 224 hair-removed image (v1: mean L*, mean b* of all pixels outside the mask) and, as a robustness variant (v2), after excluding the black dermoscope vignette (dark connected components touching the border) and a 5-px halo around the lesion, using medians. Strata used standard cut-offs (ITA > 55°, 28–55°, ≤ 28°; labelled I–II, III–IV, V–VI proxy) and are proxies, not Fitzpatrick classes. Analyses: Kruskal–Wallis tests and Holm-corrected pairwise Mann–Whitney tests [@kruskal1952,mann1947,holm1979] with rank-biserial effect sizes [@kerby2014]; Spearman correlation and OLS slope of ITA with LSAS and excess LSAS, with lesion-clustered bootstrap CIs [@efron1987]; an adjusted model of excess LSAS on ITA, log area and class; accuracy and confidence-accuracy gap by stratum with exact Clopper–Pearson intervals [@clopper1934]; and a minimum-detectable-effect calculation for the smallest stratum (two-sample, normal approximation, α = 0.05, power 0.8). The ITA distribution over all 10,015 images shows how many darker-proxy images HAM10000 offers; external resources with better darker-skin representation were assessed for feasibility (Section 4.8).")
    M.h(2, '3.12 External evaluation on PAD-UFES-20')
    M.p("PAD-UFES-20 comprises 2,298 smartphone clinical images from 1,373 patients in six diagnostic categories [@pad]. Following standard class correspondence between the two labelling schemes, ACK, BCC, MEL, NEV and SEK were mapped to AKIEC, BCC, MEL, NV and BKL respectively, and squamous cell carcinoma (SCC, 192 images) was excluded, leaving 2,106 images. DF and VASC have no PAD counterpart. The trained HAM10000 model (with its val_cal temperature) was applied without retraining. Images were pre-processed as for HAM10000 except that images with a longest side above 1,024 px were area-downscaled to 1,024 px before hair removal because full-resolution inpainting of ~10-megapixel images was computationally prohibitive; the effect of this deviation was measured by comparing predictions with and without hair removal (Section 4.9).")
    M.p("__Stepwise decomposition.__ Let Acc(X) be the accuracy on condition X. Six conditions are computed from the same saved logits, each changing one factor relative to the previous one: (A) HAM10000 test, all seven classes, seven-way output (reference); (B) HAM10000 test images of the five mappable classes only, seven-way output (changes only which images are evaluated); (C) the same images with output restricted to the five mappable classes (changes only which labels the model may choose); (P) the HAM10000 per-class recalls re-weighted to the PAD-UFES-20 class prevalence (changes only the class mix); (E) the PAD-UFES-20 images with five-way output (changes the images themselves: device, illumination, background, population and label semantics); and (D) PAD-UFES-20 with seven-way output, the original protocol. The internal-to-external drop then telescopes exactly into four components:")
    M.eq('Acc(A) − Acc(E) = [Acc(A) − Acc(B)] + [Acc(B) − Acc(C)] + [Acc(C) − Acc(P)] + [Acc(P) − Acc(E)]', 3)
    M.p("The four bracketed terms are the evaluation-population, label-space, prevalence and residual components. Each is a difference between successive conditions, so they sum to the total drop with no unexplained remainder; the residual is everything not removed by the first three steps, a composite of appearance shift and label-semantics differences (ACK versus AKIEC, SEK versus BKL) that this design cannot separate further. Macro-averaged metrics are computed over classes present in the evaluated labels, the seven-class macro-F1 that counts absent classes as zero is also reported to show its effect, and a three-class comparison (BCC, MEL, NV) confined to the classes with the closest semantic correspondence gives a bound less affected by label semantics.")
    M.p("__Covariate and adaptation diagnostics.__ A domain classifier (logistic regression on 1,024-d backbone features, 5-fold cross-validated AUROC), a Fréchet distance between feature distributions and image-statistics contrasts quantify covariate differences. An adaptation ladder on patient-level 30%/70% splits (20 repeats) estimates how much of the loss is recoverable by class-bias correction (a label-shift/prior-shift proxy [@saerens2002,lipton2018]) versus a linear probe on frozen features, which indicates whether the representation itself or only the decision layer is shifted [@morenotorres2012]. To test whether target-domain adaptation recovers the loss, the same splits were used for partial fine-tuning: dense block 4 (which holds the Grad-CAM++ target layer), the final batch normalisation and the classifier head were trained on the adaptation patients using the five mappable logits, the study's layer-wise learning rates, AdamW, 8 epochs with cosine decay and class-weighted cross-entropy, all fixed in advance, with earlier layers frozen (their outputs were computed once, so no augmentation was applied). No evaluation patient was used for any choice, all 20 splits were run for every seed model so that ladder and fine-tuning use identical patients, and zero-shot performance was recorded on the same evaluation patients before adaptation. Two zero-shot figures therefore appear in Section 4.9, the same quantity on different patient sets: the value over all mapped images ([[T:pad_decomp]]) and the mean over the 20 evaluation subsets ([[T:pad_ladder,pad_ft]]; the paired comparator for every adaptation result).")
    M.h(2, '3.13 Statistical methodology')
    M.p("Continuous outcomes were compared non-parametrically because LSAS is bounded and skewed: Kruskal–Wallis (effect size ε² = H/(n − 1) [@kelley1935]), Mann–Whitney with rank-biserial correlation [@mann1947,kerby2014], Wilcoxon signed-rank for paired differences, Spearman correlation (and rank-based partial correlation), Holm correction for pairwise families [@holm1979] and BCa bootstrap intervals for medians [@efron1987]. Lesion-clustered resampling and clustered standard errors were used wherever images of one lesion can co-occur (HAM10000 test: 990 images from 747 lesions). Because per-image statistics from different seeds are not independent (same test images), inference is within seed and consistency across seeds is reported as the range, the median p-value and the number of seeds in which a conclusion holds. Effect sizes with uncertainty are emphasized over p-values; exact p-values below 10⁻¹⁶ are reported as p < 10⁻¹⁶.")
    M.h(2, '3.14 INT8 quantization and deployment evaluation')
    M.p("__Quantization scheme.__ INT8 versions of M1 and M2 were produced with PyTorch 2.7.1 FX graph-mode quantization (torch.ao.quantization.quantize_fx; x86 backend, CPU inference) [@jacob2018,krishnamoorthi2018,nagel2021]. Weights are quantized per output channel (symmetric int8) and activations per tensor (affine uint8); convolution, batch-normalization and ReLU sequences are fused where adjacent, and the remaining batch normalizations, concatenations (DenseNet121) and residual additions (ResNet50) run as quantized operators.")
    M.p("__Configuration selection on validation data only.__ Calibration observers see only the 503 val_cal images (no labels). Candidate configurations varied the activation observer (histogram, min–max, moving-average min–max); reduce_range (on limits activations to seven bits, avoiding int16 saturation in AVX2 integer kernels on CPUs without VNNI, such as the one used here); whether the first convolution and classifier head were quantized or kept in FP32; the calibration-set size (128, 256 or 503 images); and the backend (x86, fbgemm, oneDNN). They were ranked on the 502 val_sel images by macro-F1 (differences below 0.005 treated as ties and broken by agreement with the FP32 predictions) using the seed-42 model of each architecture, and the chosen configuration was applied unchanged to all five seeds. The complete sweep is in [[T:int8_sweep]].")
    M.p("__Group-wise sensitivity and mixed precision.__ If the best post-training configuration left val_sel accuracy more than 1 percentage point, or macro-F1 more than 2 points, below the FP32 parent (a tolerance fixed before any test-set evaluation of an INT8 model, in line with the 1–2% gaps reported for 8-bit post-training and quantization-aware training [@krishnamoorthi2018]), the responsible layer groups were sought. Starting from the selected configuration, each further group (the dense blocks, transitions and final normalization of DenseNet121; the four residual stages of ResNet50) was released to FP32 one at a time and evaluated on val_sel; groups were to be added greedily while macro-F1 improved by more than 0.002 and the FP32 share of multiply-accumulates (per convolution and linear layer) stayed at or below 25%, a budget fixed in advance so that INT8's speed advantage is not traded away silently.")
    M.p("__Quantization-aware pilot.__ As a further remedy, quantization-aware refinement was run for the seed-42 model of each architecture: from the FP32 checkpoint, the network was fine-tuned with fake quantization on the training split only (same loss, sampler and augmentation; AdamW, learning rate 3 × 10⁻⁵ with cosine decay; six epochs; stem and head in FP32; observers active for the first two epochs; batch-normalization statistics frozen), the epoch with the best val_sel macro-F1 was kept, and the model was converted to a true INT8 network, to be adopted only if it improved on the best post-training variant on val_sel. Temperature scaling was refitted on the INT8 logits of val_cal for every INT8 model.")
    M.p("__Evaluation of the INT8 versions.__ INT8 and FP32 models were evaluated with identical metric code on the 990 test images and PAD-UFES-20 (Sections 3.6 and 3.12). Model size is the serialized size; CPU latency is the median of repeated forward passes at batch sizes 1 and 32 (Intel Core i5-13450HX, six threads, no other jobs running), with FP32 GPU latency (RTX 4050 Laptop) for reference. Because Grad-CAM++ needs gradients, which are undefined through integer kernels, an INT8 deployment is explained by its FP32 parent; their consistency is measured as per-image agreement of the predicted class, which fixes the class being explained.")
    M.h(2, '3.15 Model comparison and selection protocol')
    M.p("The configurations are compared on the same 990 test images and the same PAD-UFES-20 images. The criteria and decision rule were written before the results of ResNet50 seeds 44-46 and of any INT8 test evaluation were available and were not changed afterwards; only the two-seed ResNet50 results of an earlier robustness check had been seen. The criteria are: (i) discrimination, with macro-F1 as primary endpoint because it is robust to the 58:1 class imbalance, and balanced accuracy, accuracy, macro-AUROC and MCC as secondary endpoints; (ii) external generalization, the five-class PAD-UFES-20 balanced accuracy and accuracy; (iii) calibration, ECE after temperature scaling; (iv) explanation reliability, excess LSAS, insertion gain and the null-controlled gains EIG and EDG; and (v) efficiency, parameter count, serialized size and CPU latency. Between architectures (independent training runs), the five seed values of each metric are compared with Welch's t-test and an exact permutation test over the 252 possible splits of the ten values, with Cohen's d. Between a model and its INT8 version (shared weights), differences are paired by seed (mean difference with a t-based 95% confidence interval), with per-seed McNemar tests on the 990 test predictions and lesion-clustered bootstrap intervals (1,000 resamples) for differences in accuracy and macro-F1. A configuration is declared best for a criterion only if it has the highest mean and its advantage exceeds the seed standard deviation of both configurations; otherwise the criterion is a tie. No composite score is computed, because weighting the criteria is a clinical or deployment judgement this study cannot make; criterion-wise winners are reported and trade-offs discussed (Section 5.10).")
    M.h(2, '3.16 Sample size, precision and repeatability')
    M.p("__Design and unit of analysis.__ Retrospective computational evaluation of fixed public datasets. The unit of analysis is the test image (990 images from 747 lesions), with lesion-clustered resampling wherever images of one lesion co-occur; the unit of replication is the training seed (five per architecture). __Precision.__ With 990 test images the 95% confidence half-width of an accuracy near 0.81 is about 0.025 (binomial, before clustering). A paired comparison of two classifiers on the same images (McNemar test) can detect, with 80% power at α = 0.05, an accuracy difference of about 2.8 × √(ψ/n), where ψ is the proportion of discordant images (for ψ = 0.15, about 3.4 percentage points; the observed ψ is in Section 4.12). A signed-rank test of a per-image quantity such as excess LSAS can detect a standardized effect of about 0.09, and the five-versus-five comparison of seed-level means only differences of about two seed standard deviations or more (Welch test, 80% power). Effect sizes with intervals are therefore reported alongside p-values, and differences below these thresholds are described as undetected rather than absent. __Repeatability.__ The seeds, the lesion-level split (random_state = 42), the INT8 calibration subset (the first N val_cal images in metadata order) and all random baselines are fixed; software and hardware are listed in Section 7; every number in the paper is generated by build_manuscript.py from result files written by the scripts named in Section 7; and the quantized models, logits and Grad-CAM++ maps are retained. __Verification.__ The seed-level summaries (mean, median, sample SD, range, t-based 95% interval over five seeds) were recomputed once from the saved raw logits with separately written code and compared with the pipeline and every table of this paper (Section 4.15, [[T:descstats]]). These intervals describe training variability; the finite test set is covered by the binomial and lesion-clustered intervals above. __Tools.__ Python 3.12, PyTorch 2.7.1 (CUDA 11.8) with torch.ao.quantization, torchvision 0.22, pytorch-grad-cam 1.5, scikit-learn 1.8, SciPy and statsmodels 0.14.")
    M.h(2, '3.17 Mapping from claims to methods, tests and evidence')
    M.p("All thresholds used to binarize continuous measures (the STS < 0.5 instability threshold, the small/medium/large lesion-size strata and the ITA-proxy cut-points) were fixed before analysis. Multiplicity within each comparison family is controlled by the Holm procedure (Section 3.13).")
